* Exercise: Errors

Copy your ⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000001⟫Sqrt⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000002⟫ function from the [[⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000003⟫][earlier exercise]] and modify it to return an ⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000004⟫error⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000005⟫ value.

⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000006⟫Sqrt⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000007⟫ should return a non-nil error value when given a negative number, as it doesn't support complex numbers.

Create a new type

⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000008⟫

and make it an ⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000009⟫error⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000010⟫ by giving it a

⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000011⟫

method such that ⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000012⟫ErrNegativeSqrt(-2).Error()⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000013⟫ returns ⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000014⟫"cannot`Sqrt`negative`number:`-2"⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000015⟫.

⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000016⟫Note:⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000017⟫ A call to ⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000018⟫fmt.Sprint(e)⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000019⟫ inside the ⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000020⟫Error⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000021⟫ method will send the program into an infinite loop. You can avoid this by converting ⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000022⟫e⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000023⟫ first: ⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000024⟫fmt.Sprint(float64(e))⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000025⟫. Why?

Change your ⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000026⟫Sqrt⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000027⟫ function to return an ⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000028⟫ErrNegativeSqrt⟪GTI18N_41f1f733_000029⟫ value when given a negative number.

